The mechanics and the traps, verified:

```js
const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

// TRAP 1: rapid calls read stale state (batching)
// setCount(count+1); setCount(count+1); setCount(count+1);
// → count was 0 for all three → result 1, NOT 3 (verified)

// FIX: functional updates — read current at apply time
setCount(prev => prev + 1);  // 3 rapid clicks → 3 ✓

// TRAP 2: object mutation doesn't trigger re-render
// const [user, setUser] = useState({name:'A'});
// user.name = 'B'; setUser(user); — same reference → React sees no change
setUser({ ...user, name: 'B' });  // new object → re-render ✓

// TRAP 3: initializer runs on every render
const [x, setX] = useState(expensiveCompute());      // runs EVERY render
const [y, setY] = useState(() => expensiveCompute()); // lazy: runs once ✓
```

The batching model (why setters look async): React batches multiple setState calls in the same event handler into one re-render (18+: automatic batching everywhere, including Promises and timeouts). The state variable in your closure captures the value at render time — three queued updates all saw the old value. Functional updates `set(prev => next)` are the fix because the function receives the latest state when the batch applies.

The state-replacement model (the mental shift from class components): the setter REPLACES the value; it doesn't merge (unlike this.setState which shallow-mergeded). For objects/arrays you build the new value: `{...old, changed}` for objects, `[...old, item]` for arrays.

```jsx
const [count, setCount] = useState(0);
function addTwo() {
  setCount(c => c + 1);
  setCount(c => c + 1);
}
```

